steps.body:
JavaScript lets you `throw` any value: an `Error`, a string, even a plain object. TypeScript can't know which one a `catch` receives, so under `strict` the variable is `unknown`, and you narrow it before you read anything:

```typescript
try {
  throw new RangeError("quantity must be 1 to 99");
} catch (err) {
  if (err instanceof RangeError) {
    console.log(`${err.name}: ${err.message}`); // RangeError: quantity must be 1 to 99
  }
}
```

`err instanceof Error` matches errors made by this program's `Error` class and its subclasses. `Error.isError(err)`, which Node 26 supports, also recognizes errors from other realms, such as code run with `node:vm`, and isn't fooled by an object that merely has a `name` and a `message`. Both narrow `unknown` to `Error`.

Rather than repeating those checks in every `catch`, write one `toMessage(err: unknown): string` helper and call it wherever you report an error. Catch only what you can handle, and rethrow the rest with `throw err`, so a real bug still surfaces.

examples.code:
function toMessage(err: unknown): string {
  if (err instanceof Error) {
    return err.message;
  }
  if (typeof err === "string") {
    return err;
  }
  return `unexpected value: ${JSON.stringify(err)}`;
}

const failures: (() => void)[] = [
  () => {
    throw new RangeError("quantity must be 1 to 99");
  },
  () => {
    throw "disk full";
  },
  () => {
    throw { code: 503 };
  },
];

for (const fail of failures) {
  try {
    fail();
  } catch (err) {
    console.log(toMessage(err));
  }
}

examples.output:
quantity must be 1 to 99
disk full
unexpected value: {"code":503}

